@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Index Performance";
}

<div class="container-fluid px-4 py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Index Performance
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how indexes improve query performance and how to
            maintain them effectively in SQL Server.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is Index Performance?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Index performance refers to how effectively indexes help
                SQL Server find and retrieve data from a table.
            </p>

            <p>
                Properly designed indexes can significantly improve
                <strong>SELECT</strong> query performance.
            </p>

            <p>
                However, unnecessary or poorly designed indexes can
                increase storage usage and slow down
                <strong>INSERT</strong>, <strong>UPDATE</strong> and
                <strong>DELETE</strong> operations.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHY INDEXES IMPROVE PERFORMANCE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Why Do Indexes Improve Performance?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Without a suitable index, SQL Server may need to examine
                many or all rows in a table to find the required data.
            </p>

            <p>
                This operation is commonly known as a
                <strong>Table Scan</strong>.
            </p>

            <p>
                With a suitable index, SQL Server may be able to locate
                the required rows more efficiently.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TABLE SCAN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Table Scan
            </h2>

            <p>
                A table scan occurs when SQL Server reads rows throughout
                a table to find matching records.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                If there is no useful index on the City column,
                SQL Server may need to scan a large portion of the table.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INDEX SEEK
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Index Seek
            </h2>

            <p>
                An <strong>Index Seek</strong> allows SQL Server to
                navigate directly to the relevant portion of an index
                instead of examining every row.
            </p>

            <p>
                Index seeks are generally efficient when a suitable
                index exists and the query can use it effectively.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example of Index Performance
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we have the following table:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Course VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Create an index on City:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students(City);</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Now execute:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                SQL Server can consider the City index when creating
                the execution plan for this query.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTION PLAN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Execution Plan
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server uses an <strong>Execution Plan</strong> to
                determine how a query should be executed.
            </p>

            <p>
                The execution plan can show whether SQL Server is using
                operations such as:
            </p>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Index Seek
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Index Scan
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Table Scan
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Key Lookup
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DISPLAY ACTUAL EXECUTION PLAN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Viewing Execution Plan in SSMS
            </h2>

            <p>
                In SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS), you can enable
                the Actual Execution Plan before running a query.
            </p>

            <p>
                Keyboard shortcut:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>Ctrl + M</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                After enabling it, execute the query and open the
                <strong>Execution Plan</strong> tab to analyze how
                SQL Server processed the query.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INDEX SCAN VS SEEK
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-4">
                Index Seek vs Index Scan
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>

                            <th>
                                Index Seek
                            </th>

                            <th>
                                Index Scan
                            </th>

                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Searches specific portions of an index.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Reads a larger portion or the entire index.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Usually efficient for selective queries.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Can be useful when many rows are required.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Typically accesses fewer rows.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                May process many rows.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INDEX SELECTIVITY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Index Selectivity
            </h2>

            <p>
                Selectivity describes how effectively an index can
                distinguish between rows.
            </p>

            <p>
                Columns with many distinct values often have higher
                selectivity than columns with only a few distinct values.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, a StudentId column usually has better
                selectivity than a Gender column.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INDEXED COLUMNS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Choosing Columns for Indexes
            </h2>

            <p>
                Good candidates for indexes are columns frequently used
                in:
            </p>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    WHERE conditions
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    JOIN conditions
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ORDER BY
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    GROUP BY
                </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
                However, indexes should be created based on actual
                query and workload requirements.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         COVERING INDEX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Covering Index
            </h2>

            <p>
                A covering index contains all the columns required by
                a query, allowing SQL Server to retrieve the required
                information directly from the index.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students(City)
INCLUDE(StudentName, Course);</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                In this example, City is the index key while
                StudentName and Course are included columns.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TOO MANY INDEXES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Problem with Too Many Indexes
            </h2>

            <p>
                Creating an index for every column is not recommended.
            </p>

            <p>
                Each additional index requires storage and may need
                maintenance whenever data changes.
            </p>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    More storage usage
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Slower INSERT operations
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Additional UPDATE maintenance
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Additional DELETE maintenance
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INDEX FRAGMENTATION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Index Fragmentation
            </h2>

            <p>
                Index fragmentation can occur as rows are inserted,
                updated and deleted over time.
            </p>

            <p>
                Fragmentation can affect the efficiency of index access,
                especially in heavily modified tables.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CHECK FRAGMENTATION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Checking Index Fragmentation
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server provides
                <strong>sys.dm_db_index_physical_stats</strong>
                to inspect index physical statistics.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    OBJECT_NAME(object_id) AS TableName,
    index_id,
    avg_fragmentation_in_percent
FROM sys.dm_db_index_physical_stats
(
    DB_ID(),
    NULL,
    NULL,
    NULL,
    'LIMITED'
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         REBUILD INDEX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Rebuilding an Index
            </h2>

            <p>
                An index can be rebuilt to reorganize its structure.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students
REBUILD;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Rebuilding an index can be useful when significant
                fragmentation needs to be addressed.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         REORGANIZE INDEX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Reorganizing an Index
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server also supports index reorganization.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students
REORGANIZE;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Reorganize is an online operation that incrementally
                defragments an index.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         REBUILD VS REORGANIZE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-4">
                REBUILD vs REORGANIZE
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>REBUILD</th>
                            <th>REORGANIZE</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Recreates the index structure.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Defragments the existing index incrementally.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Can be more resource intensive.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Generally less resource intensive.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Useful when substantial maintenance is required.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Useful for lighter fragmentation.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BEST PRACTICES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Index Performance Best Practices
            </h2>

            <ol>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Create indexes based on actual query requirements.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid creating unnecessary indexes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Monitor query performance regularly.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Review execution plans for important queries.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Monitor index fragmentation where appropriate.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Maintain indexes according to workload requirements.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Consider covering indexes for frequently executed queries.
                </li>

            </ol>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                Indexes can improve SELECT query performance.
            </li>

            <li>
                Index Seek is generally efficient for selective searches.
            </li>

            <li>
                Table Scan may occur when a suitable index is unavailable.
            </li>

            <li>
                Too many indexes can negatively affect write operations.
            </li>

            <li>
                Execution Plans help analyze query performance.
            </li>

            <li>
                Index fragmentation should be monitored when appropriate.
            </li>

            <li>
                REBUILD and REORGANIZE are available for index maintenance.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="p-4 rounded shadow-sm mb-4"
         style="background-color:#cfe2ff;">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p>
            Indexes are an important part of SQL Server performance
            optimization. A properly designed index can reduce the amount
            of data SQL Server needs to examine when executing queries.
        </p>

        <p>
            Execution plans can be used to understand how SQL Server
            accesses data and whether operations such as Index Seek,
            Index Scan or Table Scan are being used.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Indexes should be created and maintained carefully because
            they require storage and can increase the cost of data
            modification operations.
        </p>

    </div>


